1. What is Server Response
A server response is the data or message sent by a server to a client after receiving a request.
In Node.js, it is handled using the res object in the http.createServer() method.
Response can be:

Plain text
HTML
JSON (data objects)

const http = require("http");

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    // Set response header
    res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });

    // Send plain text response
    res.write("Hello from Node.js Server\n");

    // Send variable data
    let username = "Bhartee";
    res.write(`Username is: ${username}\n`);

    // Send function data
    function greet(name) {
        return `Welcome, ${name}!`;
    }
    res.write(greet(username));

    // End the response
    res.end();
});

server.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running on http://localhost:3000");
});


Explanation:
res.writeHead() → sets HTTP status and headers
res.write() → sends data in response
res.end() → ends the response

3. Passing Variable and Function Data
You can pass variables and function return values inside res.write().
Example above shows:
Variable: username
Function: greet(username)


4. Interview Notes / Points
Server response is sent using res object.
res.writeHead(statusCode, headers) sets response metadata.
res.write(data) sends data in chunks.
res.end() closes the response.
Can send text, HTML, JSON. Example for JSON:
res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "application/json" });
res.end(JSON.stringify({ name: "Bhartee", age: 29 }));
Node.js is event-driven, so server can handle multiple requests asynchronously.
Variables and functions can be passed dynamically in the response.